 about how we could achieve this goal. First of all, let us take a look at the architecture of our network. We have two hidden layers for both the policy and value functions. The activation function used is tanh. Now, let us try to reduce the number of parameters without compromising on the performance of the model. One way to do this would be to use fewer hidden layers or smaller hidden units. However, since we want to retain the same performance, we will need to experiment with different combinations of hidden layer sizes and activations until we find one that works well. Another approach could be to use a more complex activation function such as ReLU instead of tanh which might lead to better results. Finally, we could also consider using dropout regularization during training which helps prevent overfitting by randomly dropping out some neurons during backpropagation. Overall, there are many ways to achieve this goal but it depends on what kind of trade-offs you are willing to make between accuracy and computational efficiency.

