 about how we could modify the above code to make it more efficient. First, let's look at the `GenePolicy` class definition. We see that it inherits from `ActorCriticPolicy`, which means that it will have access to all the features provided by this base class. However, we also notice that there are some differences between our proposed policy and the one used in the original implementation. For instance, instead of using ReLU activations, we use Tanh. This choice may seem arbitrary but it has been shown to lead to better performance in certain tasks. Another difference lies in the architecture of the neural network used for both the actor and critic networks. Instead of having two separate layers for each network, we combine them into one single layer with a shared set of weights. This approach allows us to reduce the number of parameters while still maintaining good generalization capabilities. Finally, note that we don't specify any specific loss functions or optimization algorithms - these details will be handled automatically by the underlying framework (in this case, Stable Baselines).

Now let's move on to the `get_policy_kwargs()` function. Here again, we see that we're defining a dictionary containing several key-value pairs. The keys correspond to different hyperparameters that can be tuned during training. In particular, we've included the following options:

* `learning_rate`: The learning rate used for updating the model parameters.
* `gamma`: The discount factor applied when computing the returns.
* `gae_lambda`: A parameter controlling the trade-off between immediate rewards and future rewards.
* `clip_range`: The maximum magnitude of the clipped gradients.
* `ent_coef`: The coefficient multiplying the entropy term in the objective function.
* `vf_coef`: The coefficient multiplying the value function loss term.
* `max_grad_norm`: The maximum norm allowed for the gradient updates.
* `n_steps`: The number of timesteps over which to average the advantages.
* `batch_size`: The size of the minibatch used for stochastic gradient descent.
* `n_epochs`: The number of epochs performed per update step.
* `normalize_advantage`: Whether to normalize the advantage estimates before computing the loss.
* `verbose`: The level of verbosity desired during training.

Overall, these hyperparameters provide a wide range of possibilities for fine-tuning the algorithm according to the specific requirements of the problem at hand. By modifying just a few lines of code, we were able to significantly enhance the performance of the baseline model without compromising its simplicity and flexibility.

